  "preamble": "Change of a rhotic to a voiced velar plosive [g]. Best known from the Philippines: Proto-Austronesian/Proto-Philippine *R, a rhotic of uncertain value (often taken as a uvular trill), became g in Tagalog, Cebuano, Ilocano and Bikol. Other Philippine languages have y, l, h or r for the same sound, a split known as the RGH law and used for subgrouping.",
